{"decision":"FIX","summary":"The fallback is a bounded, generally applicable post-rerank safety mechanism and its focused tests exercise the stated control/bore/valve conflict scenarios. However, the change accidentally disables existing `QueryConstraints` validation: the `dn` and `pn_min_mpa` validators are now nested under `SelectionCompatibility`, so negative values are accepted by `QueryConstraints`.","issues":["Regression: `QueryConstraints.model_validate({\"product_type\":\"ball_valve\",\"dn\":-1,\"pn_min_mpa\":-0.1})` accepts both invalid values. Move `_validate_dn` and `_validate_pn` back into `QueryConstraints`; they are currently indented inside `SelectionCompatibility`.","Add a regression test asserting that non-positive `dn` and `pn_min_mpa` are rejected by `QueryConstraints`.","Local pytest could not run in this read-only sandbox because no usable temporary directory is available; the validation regression was verified directly through model construction."],"fix_plan":["Move the two Pydantic field validators immediately after the `QueryConstraints` fields, before the new dataclasses.","Remove those validator methods from `SelectionCompatibility`.","Add focused invalid-value tests, then rerun the focused and full test suites in a normal writable environment."],"next_direction":"After restoring the unrelated validation regression, measure this candidate normally. The approach is worth evaluating: it is fail-open, uses only retrieved candidates, and requires explicit evidence for a replacement; inspect fallback provenance and any false-match movement before considering broader structured selection rules."}
